    "keywords": "Georgia Personal Injury Legal Education Center | Free Legal Resources Georgia Personal Injury Education Center What Every Georgia Accident Victim Should Know What to Do After a Car Accident in Georgia Georgia's 2-Year Statute of Limitations Explained Georgia Comparative Negligence: How It Affects Your Case What Is My Car Accident Claim Worth in Georgia? How Insurance Adjusters Lowball Settlements: And How to Fight Back 10 Costly Mistakes to Avoid After a Georgia Accident Post-Accident Checklist for Georgia Drivers Georgia Personal Injury Questions Answered Explore More From Awad Law Firm Talk to an Experienced Personal Injury Attorney Today Subscribe to our Newsletter Georgia Personal Injury Legal Education Center | Free Legal Resources: Awad Law Firm Home About About Mission & Vision Firm History Ibrahim Awad Team Members Why Choose Us Core Values Community Practice Areas Practice Areas Personal Injury Car Accidents Trucking Accidents Motorcycle Accidents Bicycle Accidents Uber Accidents Lyft Accidents Slip & Fall Medical Malpractice Wrongful Death Results Results Case Studies Video Testimonials Reviews Resources Resources TEDx Talk Legal Education Newsletter Awad Academy Newsletter Contact Search here... Free Consultation Home Resources Education Free Legal Resources For Georgia Residents Georgia Personal Injury Education Center Know your rights before the insurance company does. Our attorneys share the same knowledge they use in court: free, in plain English, for every Georgian who needs it. Explore the Guides Jump to FAQ 2 Yrs Georgia Statute of Limitations &lt;50% Max fault to still recover damages $0 Upfront cost to consult attorneys KNOWLEDGE IS POWER Legal Guides What Every Georgia Accident Victim Should Know Georgia personal injury law is full of traps for the uninformed. These attorney-authored guides answer the questions we hear every day. Crash Protocol What to Do After a Car Accident in Georgia The first 48 hours after a crash determine whether you win or lose your claim. Here's exactly what to do: and what never to say. Read Guide Statutes & Rules Georgia's 2-Year Statute of Limitations Explained Miss this deadline and your case is gone forever. Learn how O.C.G.A. &sect; 9-3-33 works, what exceptions exist, and why delay is dangerous. Read Guide Fault Mechanics Georgia Comparative Negligence: How It Affects Your Case Insurers use Georgia's modified comparative fault rule to slash settlements. Know how it works before they use it against you. Read Guide Valuation Rules What Is My Car Accident Claim Worth in Georgia? Economic damages, pain and suffering, punitive awards: here's how Georgia courts and insurers calculate the value of your claim. Read Guide Negotiation Tech How Insurance Adjusters Lowball Settlements: And How to Fight Back Recorded statements, quick-offer tactics, blame-shifting: learn the insurance adjuster playbook and how Awad Law Firm counters every move. Read Guide Mistakes to Avoid 10 Costly Mistakes to Avoid After a Georgia Accident Many Georgia accident victims unknowingly destroy their own claims. These are the ten most damaging errors: and exactly how to avoid them. Read Guide Post-Accident Checklist for Georgia Drivers Our attorneys condensed everything you need to do in the first 72 hours into a single, printable checklist. Keep it in your glove box before you ever need it. Request Your Free Checklist QUESTIONS ANSWERED FAQ Hub Georgia Personal Injury Questions Answered Straight answers from a Marietta personal injury attorney: not a chatbot. How long do I have to file a personal injury claim in Georgia? In Georgia, you typically have two years from the date of the accident to file a personal injury lawsuit (O.C.G.A. &sect; 9-3-33). However, there are exceptions: such as claims against government entities, which require formal notices within 6 to 12 months. Delaying can result in lost evidence, so consulting an attorney early is highly recommended. What is Georgia's comparative negligence rule? Georgia operates under a modified comparative negligence system. This means you can still recover damages as long as you are less than 50% at fault for the accident. However, your total financial recovery will be reduced by your exact percentage of fault. For example, if you are awarded $100,000 but found 20% at fault, you will take home $80,000. Do I need a lawyer for a car accident claim in Georgia? While minor \"fender benders\" without physical injuries may be settled without legal counsel, any crash involving injuries, medical treatment, or lost wages warrants an attorney's guidance. Insurance adjusters are trained to minimize payouts; an experienced lawyer protects your interests, calculates long-term medical needs, and aggressively negotiates the highest possible recovery. What damages can I recover after a Georgia accident? You may be eligible to recover three types of damages: Economic Damages (medical bills, future clinical care, lost wages, and property damage), Non-Economic Damages (pain and suffering, emotional distress, and loss of life quality), and in rare cases of extreme recklessness (like DUI), Punitive Damages designed to punish the at-fault party. What should I do immediately after a car accident in Georgia? Immediately ensure your physical safety and dial 911 for law enforcement and medical support. Document the scene by taking comprehensive photographs of vehicle damage, license plates, and surrounding road signs. Gather contact details from witnesses. Seek clinical evaluation even if you feel fine, and contact Awad Law Firm before making statements to any insurance adjusters. How much does hiring a personal injury attorney in Georgia cost? Hiring us costs absolutely nothing out of pocket. We work on a strict contingency fee arrangement, which means our fee is entirely tied to a successful financial settlement or jury award. If we do not recover money for your claim, you owe us absolutely nothing in fees. Can I still recover damages if I was partly at fault in Georgia? Yes. Under O.C.G.A. &sect; 51-12-33, as long as you are found to be 49% or less responsible for the crash, you have a legal right to seek partial "
