    "keywords": "article distracted driver Injured by a Distracted Driver In Georgia? Here Is How to Fight Back Georgia's Hands-Free Law (O.C.G.A. &sect; 40-6-241) Explained Five Tactical Ways We Prove Driver Distraction Recovering Punitive Damages in Egregious Distraction Cases Hold Negligent Distracted Drivers Accountable article distracted driver | The Awad Law Firm Home About About Mission & Vision Firm History Ibrahim Awad Team Members Why Choose Us Core Values Community Practice Areas Practice Areas Personal Injury Car Accidents Trucking Accidents Motorcycle Accidents Bicycle Accidents Uber Accidents Lyft Accidents Slip & Fall Medical Malpractice Wrongful Death Results Results Case Studies Video Testimonials Reviews Resources Resources TEDx Talk Legal Education Newsletter Awad Academy Newsletter Contact Search here... Free Consultation Liability & Negligence Injured by a Distracted Driver In Georgia? Here Is How to Fight Back Texting, browsing, and phone usage behind the wheel cause severe collisions. Learn your rights under GA hands-free laws and how we prove distraction. NEGLIGENCE RECOVERY By Amir Makled, Esq. &bull; Staging SEO Publication Distracted driving is one of the leading causes of catastrophic motor vehicle crashes on Georgia roads today. Every time a motorist looks down at a phone screen to read a text message, change a playlist, or check social media, they are driving blindly at highway speeds. If you have been injured in a collision caused by a motorist's focus failure, establishing distraction is crucial to securing maximum personal injury compensation. In this guide, we outline Georgia's strict Hands-Free Law, explain the legal methods used to prove electronic distraction, and discuss how establishing fault can drastically maximize the average car accident settlement in Georgia you receive. Georgia's Hands-Free Law (O.C.G.A. &sect; 40-6-241) Explained Passed in 2018, Georgia's Hands-Free Law establishes clear, strict guidelines governing device usage behind the wheel. Under the law, drivers are strictly forbidden from: Forbidden Actions Physically holding or supporting a phone with any part of their body. Writing, sending, or reading text messages, emails, or internet content. Watching videos, recording footage, or browsing social media apps. Allowed Actions (With Hands-Free) Speaking or texting using voice-to-text systems. Using earpieces, smartwatches, or integrated Bluetooth systems to talk. Using GPS navigation apps, provided the device is mounted. Violation of the Hands-Free Georgia Act is considered a misdemeanor and carries fines and points on the at-fault driver's license. More importantly, in a personal injury lawsuit, a hands-free violation constitutes Negligence Per Se : meaning the driver is automatically presumed negligent under Georgia law because they violated a safety statute. According to safety data from the NHTSA , mobile distraction increases your accident risk by over 400%: Struck by a Distracted Driver? Prove It! Proving that the at-fault driver was holding a phone requires fast, professional legal action before data logs are lost or overwritten. Speak with Attorney Amir Makled 24/7. Start My Case Free &rarr; Call (706) 890-0000 Five Tactical Ways We Prove Driver Distraction Insurance companies representing at-fault drivers will rarely admit their driver was distracted. Proving electronic distraction requires aggressive investigation and early preservation of evidence: 1 Subpoenaing Cell Phone Records By issuing a formal legal subpoena to the at-fault driver's cell provider, we can obtain raw call logs, text timestamps, and data usage statistics matching the exact minute of the collision. 2 Analyzing Police Reports & Citations Responding officers will inspect device screens and interview at-fault drivers. If the officer writes a citation under O.C.G.A. § 40-6-241, it establishes a powerful record of negligence. 3 Witness Statements Bystanders, passengers, and other motorists frequently witness at-fault drivers looking down, texting, or typing just before a collision. Sworn witness statements provide crucial leverage. 4 Dashcam & Traffic Footage Many commercial vehicles and private cars carry dashcams. Surrounding store security feeds or local traffic cameras can provide objective visual proof of the driver's phone usage. 5 Black Box (EDR) Data Extraction Event Data Recorders (EDRs) track speed, steering adjustments, and braking patterns. Distracted drivers typically fail to brake or steer prior to impact, establishing a lack of situational awareness. Recovering Punitive Damages in Egregious Distraction Cases In standard personal injury claims, you recover compensatory damages (medical costs, lost wages, pain and suffering). However, under Georgia code ( O.C.G.A. § 51-12-5.1 ), if the driver's conduct demonstrates a conscious indifference to consequences, you may be awarded Punitive Damages . In cases where a commercial truck driver or motorist was actively streaming video, texting extensively, or browsing websites while driving at high speed, Georgia courts have held that this rises to the level of willful misconduct, enabling additional punitive awards designed to punish the driver and deter others. Hold Negligent Distracted Drivers Accountable If you have been injured by a distracted driver, do not let their insurance company sweep the phone usage under the rug. Contact The Awad Law Firm today for a 24/7 free consultation. Start My Evaluation Call (706) 890-0000 Aggressive personal injury litigation and relentless advocacy across the state of Georgia. Practice Areas Car Accidents Truck Accidents Motorcycle Accidents Wrongful Death Slip & Fall Personal Injury Resources About Our Firm Ibrahim Awad Bio Our Services Our Results Education Center Newsletter Contact Us Phone ("
